Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, minimalmodbus, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError() and .registers; pyModbusTCP returns lists directly or None on failure -- error handling 
    │   logic must be rewritten; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RTU/ASCII) communication; TCP support is limited/less idiomatic, and it raises
    │   exceptions instead of returning error-checkable objects; modbus-tk uses a master.execute() call pattern with modbus_tcp.TcpMaster, quite different from
    │   pymodbus's client.read_holding_registers signature; unit/slave id parameter naming and position differs across libraries (unit= vs slaveaddress= vs 
    │   slave=); connection lifecycle management (connect/close) differs between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages implement Modbus master/client functionality capable of reading and writing holding registers over TCP or serial, which is the only 
        operation this file performs (read_holding_registers/write_register). pyModbusTCP is a close functional match (TCP-only client with read/write register
        methods). minimalmodbus and modbus-tk are also Modbus master libraries but use different connection models (serial-first for minimalmodbus, 
        hook/callback style for modbus-tk) and different return/error conventions than pymodbus's isError()-based result objects.

